    "q-332436": {
      "dom_id": "step-q332436",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{4}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-6},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{4}{s}-\\frac{1}{s-6}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">\n$x(t)=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n</td>\n\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">\n$\\,\\,{e}^{6t}$\n</td>\n\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{4}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & =4{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+{e}^{6\\cdot t} \\\\  & =4+{e}^{6t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{4}{s}-\\frac{1}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & =4{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-{e}^{6\\cdot t} \\\\  & =4-{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4+{e}^{6t} \\\\ 4-{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
